{"episodes":{"187995":"Unseasonably warm weather spread across northwestern Wisconsin on the 3rd ahead of a cold front moving into the region. Showers and thunderstorms developed ahead of the front during the late afternoon and early evening hours and moved to the northeast. Cold temperatures aloft allowed the storms to produce small hail as they moved through. One storm in western Bayfield County did produce hail to the size of quarters during the early evening hours.","188035":"Strong to severe thunderstorms developed along a warm front producing large hail and a gustnado.","188305":"Strong to severe thunderstorms developed along a warm front producing large hail.","188977":"Scattered thunderstorms moved across parts of northern Illinois during the afternoon and evening of March 4th producing large hail.","189142":"A cold front moved into Arkansas from the Southern Plains. Temperatures across Arkansas were in the mid 70s and low 80s on the afternoon of March 4th. A handful of thunderstorms developed ahead of the front in which a few became severe. Large hail and damaging winds were common with the storms that became severe across the central and eastern portions of the state.","189644":"Scattered storms developed during the afternoon of March 4th, with numerous storms throughout the area by the evening. Most storms produced small hail, with several storms producing upwards to one-inch hail or larger. The largest hail seen was golf ball-sized in northeast Missouri and southern Iowa. One brief tornado was also seen, southeast of Pearl City Illinois, with no damage observed.","189645":"Scattered storms developed during the afternoon of March 4th, with numerous storms throughout the area by the evening. Most storms produced small hail, with several storms producing upwards to one-inch hail or larger. The largest hail seen was golf ball-sized in northeast Missouri and southern Iowa. One brief tornado was also seen, southeast of Pearl City Illinois, with no damage observed.","189646":"Scattered storms developed during the afternoon of March 4th, with numerous storms throughout the area by the evening. Most storms produced small hail, with several storms producing upwards to one-inch hail or larger. The largest hail seen was golf ball-sized in northeast Missouri and southern Iowa. One brief tornado was also seen, southeast of Pearl City Illinois, with no damage observed.","190009":"The combination of increasing surface heating, elevated moisture, and increasing atmospheric life from an approaching mid level disturbance induced increasing storm development. The main focus of heavier rain was on the south shore of Lake Pontchartrain. Slow storm movement and moderate to heavy rain rates resulted in multiple areas of flash flooding across New Orleans Metro.","190010":"The combination of increasing surface heating, elevated moisture, and increasing atmospheric life from an approaching mid level disturbance induced increasing storm development. One storm was able to reach severe limits over the middle of Lake Pontchartrain.","190212":"Numerous showers and thunderstorms developed across portions of Deep East Texas, North & Central Louisiana, and Southern Arkansas during the late morning hours on March 4th, and continued well into the late afternoon and early evening as a strong upper level disturbance moved across the region. With low wet-bulb zero heights, steep mid-level lapse rates, and sufficient effective bulk shear, the atmosphere was conducive for storms to produce hail. Several reports of 1 inch hail were received, mainly across portions of Northeast and Central Louisiana.","190496":"A deep trough brought widespread hail showers and snow to Northwest California. Heavy snow of several feet was measured in the mountains while the hail showers resulted in numerous traffic accidents along the coast.","190786":"A few strong to severe thunderstorms moved across northeast Louisiana during the afternoon of March 4th."},"events":[{"begin":